  "synonym_discrimination": {
    "words": [
      "go-v",
      "come-v"
    ],
    "note": "Both are verbs of movement, and the direction decides between them. **Go** is movement away from where the speaker is: *I'm going to the store.* **Come** is movement toward the speaker, toward the listener, or to a place where the two will be together: *come here*; *I'll come to your party*; *are you coming with us?* For the same reason, **has gone** means is still away and **has been** means went and came back."
  },
  "usage_note": "In speech, **go** is often followed by **and** plus another verb: *go and see who it is.* American English also uses the bare verb, **go see**, **go get**, but only after the base form **go**: *went and saw*, never *went saw*.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "I go to home at six.",
      "correct": "I go home at six.",
      "note": "**Home** is an adverb after **go**, so there is no **to**; other places take **to**: *go to work*, *go to the office*."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "Let's go to shopping.",
      "correct": "Let's go shopping.",
      "note": "Activities take the -ing form after **go**, with no **to**: *go swimming*, *go fishing*."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "I have gone to that restaurant many times.",
      "correct": "I have been to that restaurant many times.",
      "note": "**Has gone** means the person is still away; **has been** means they went and came back."
    }
  ],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "**Go** is the general word for moving away from the speaker; **come** is toward. Beyond movement, **go** covers leaving, working (of machines), passing (of time), becoming (of bad changes), and the future (**be going to**). Most of these are separate words in other languages.",
    "grammar": "No preposition before **home**, **there**, **abroad**, or **upstairs** (*go home*). Activities take the -ing form (*go shopping*), not **to** plus a verb. The present perfect uses two verbs for a completed trip: **has gone** (still away); **has been**, using **be** not **go**, means went and returned. **Be going to** plus a base verb is the spoken future.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "The vowel is the long **o** of **no**, a moving sound that ends with rounded lips. **Goes** rhymes with **nose**, and the past tense **went** is an unrelated form that must be learned. **Gone** rhymes with **on** in American English."
  },
